Runbook
=======

The two sequences that actually get used, written down because the order matters
and because getting it wrong produces failures that name the wrong thing.

Every command below is given twice: as it is typed on the box, and as it is
driven from the development machine through the transfer agent. The second form
is what most of this project has been done with.

Two rules that apply to everything here, both learned expensively:

  * Anything long-lived is launched with `-r 0` and never waited on. The
    transfer agent retries a command it believes timed out, and a daemon never
    returns, so without `-r 0` one launch becomes up to three -- two slirp
    bridges competing for one guest's rings, two debug daemons splitting the
    log. Each copy also holds a driver handle, so `sc stop` parks in
    STOP_PENDING and the driver file cannot be replaced.
  * `xstart.bat` is the exception that returns. `start` hands its console
    handles to the child whatever you do with them, so a batch file that uses it
    wedges its caller forever; xstart.bat goes through WScript.Shell instead.


A. Clean box to a running desktop
---------------------------------

Starting point: the box is powered on, no coLinux processes, driver STOPPED.
`tools/wol.py` will power it on if it is off.

On the box, from F:\xfer\mocolinux-m2:

    colinux-daemon.exe --install-driver     # idempotent; only creates the service
    sc start CoLinuxDriver                  # this is what actually loads it
    dbg-start.bat                           # optional; see note below
    run-manjaro.bat                         # the guest. Never returns.
    net.bat                                 # slirp. Never returns.
    xstart.bat                              # X server. Returns immediately.
    console.bat                             # console on 2323. Never returns.

From the development machine:

    X=python3 /path/to/xpc.py
    $X -r 0 exec -T 60 -C mocolinux-m2 -- colinux-daemon.exe --install-driver
    $X -r 0 exec -T 60 cmd /c "sc start CoLinuxDriver | findstr STATE"
    ( $X -r 0 exec -T 7200 -C mocolinux-m2 cmd /c dbg-start.bat > logs/dbg.log 2>&1 & )
    ( $X -r 0 exec -T 7200 -C mocolinux-m2 -- cmd /c run-manjaro.bat > logs/boot.log 2>&1 & )
    ( $X -r 0 exec -T 7200 -C mocolinux-m2 -- cmd /c net.bat > logs/net.log 2>&1 & )
    $X -r 0 exec -T 45 -C mocolinux-m2 cmd /c xstart.bat
    ( $X -r 0 exec -T 7200 -C mocolinux-m2 -- cmd /c console.bat > logs/console.log 2>&1 & )

Then connect, and start something:

    python3 tools/coterm.py 'uptime; systemctl is-system-running'
    python3 tools/coterm.py '( setsid konsole > ~/k.log 2>&1 & )'

Notes that save time:

  * The guest takes roughly half a minute to answer. `colinux-daemon.exe`
    reaching about 57 MB in the task list means the image is loaded and the
    guest is live; before that it sits near 10 MB. A console attempt that is too
    early reports "connection refused" (console.bat has not bound yet) or hangs
    with no prompt (the guest has not reached one). Neither is a failure.
  * Order matters in one place only: the X server must exist before an X client
    starts, and an X client with no server produces no error at all -- it simply
    never appears. That cost an hour once.
  * The guest configures its own network at boot. There is nothing to type; if
    it were missing the symptom would be "cannot resolve host", which reads as
    DNS rather than as a missing bridge.
  * The debug daemon streams the driver's records to the development machine as
    they are produced, so they survive a host reset. That is the only instrument
    that says anything when the machine dies hard, and it is how both the
    guest-RAM allocation freeze and the 32-bit triple fault were localised. It
    is also, currently, able to bugcheck the machine itself (see TODO), so it is
    started when something is being hunted and left off otherwise.

Ending a session:

    python3 tools/coterm.py 'sudo systemctl poweroff'     # if the filesystem matters
    colinux-daemon.exe --stop                             # stop.bat: ends a wedged run

`poweroff` and nothing else when anything was written. `stop.bat` and the run
deadline do not unmount, and ext4 in data=ordered journals metadata but not
contents, so a file written seconds earlier comes back as a correctly-named
inode with no data. The console and the bridge exit on their own once the guest
is gone; the driver then unloads cleanly with `sc stop CoLinuxDriver`.

Recovering a box that has stopped answering. Three deaths, three recoveries,
and telling them apart saves a walk to the machine:

  * **Triple fault** -- the processor resets immediately. No bugcheck, no dump,
    nothing in any log. The machine comes back on its own; wait for it.
  * **Bugcheck** -- the kernel stops and waits, powered on, showing a screen.
    `tools/autoreboot.bat` (run once per install) makes it write a minidump and
    reboot instead. Nothing is lost by that: the code and parameters are in the
    dump, which getdumps.bat collects, and every bugcheck this project has
    diagnosed was read from a dump rather than off the screen.
  * **Hard hang** -- powered, nothing executing. Only the power button.

`tools/wol.py` distinguishes the first from the other two for free. A cleanly
powered-off box answers about twenty seconds after the magic packet; if it does
not, the machine is powered ON and not running, because the NIC only arms its
magic-packet filter in a real power-off state. WoL failing is therefore evidence,
not a broken tool.

`tools/hardreset.exe` covers the case where Windows still schedules but
`shutdown.exe` cannot finish -- a thread blocked inside our driver. See the
comment at the top of tools/hardreset.cs; it goes through NtShutdownSystem so
nothing in user mode is asked to co-operate, and forcing the user-mode path
instead is the documented way to produce this port's recurring 0x50.

And the one that governs the whole day: **the first boot after a host reboot is
reliable and the second is not.** Guest RAM is freed and re-hunted in unbroken
32 MB runs every run, so the second boot's allocations grind the host until it
is power-cycled, with memory showing as available throughout. Reboot between
runs until that is fixed (TODO has the fix).


B. Clean host to an installed Manjaro
-------------------------------------

Starting point: the box has the driver and the binaries, a `vmlinux`, and an
existing bootable guest of some kind. That last requirement is the awkward part
and is discussed at the end.

1. Make the disk on the box. Instantly, and without transferring anything:

       fsutil file createnew F:\xfer\mocolinux-m2\root-manjaro.img 32212254720

   A 30 GB image is created in place. Do not build it on the development machine
   and push it: the agent buffers the whole file and deploy verifies by reading
   it back, which is tens of gigabytes of churn on a 4 GB machine and has taken
   the box down.

2. Boot a guest that has the new disk attached as a second unit. `run-arch.bat`
   does exactly this -- the old Arch root on cobd0, the target on cobd1 --
   together with net.bat and console.bat as in section A.

3. Get the script into the guest. It is in the tree, so serve it over the link:

       ( cd tools && python3 -m http.server 8099 --bind 192.168.137.1 & )
       python3 tools/coterm.py 'curl -4 -s -o /root/mk.sh http://192.168.137.1:8099/mkmanjarorootfs.sh'

4. Run it against the raw device, detached, because it takes twenty minutes and
   the console must stay usable:

       python3 tools/coterm.py '( sh /root/mk.sh /dev/cobd1 > /root/mk.out 2>&1; echo "RC=$?" >> /root/mk.out ) & echo STARTED'

   Progress: `grep "==>" /root/mk.out`. It reports each stage, and the last line
   is either "done, unmounted clean" or a warning that it could not unmount --
   which matters, because an unflushed image is a tree of correct filenames with
   no contents.

5. Verify before trusting it. This is not optional; a block-layer bug once
   produced an image whose own package database was binary noise:

       python3 tools/coterm.py 'mount /dev/cobd1 /mnt/new && pacman --root /mnt/new -Qk | grep -v "0 missing files"'

   Silence is a pass. Then `umount /mnt/new` and `sudo systemctl poweroff`.

6. Boot it: `run-manjaro.bat`, which puts the new image on cobd0 and keeps the
   old one on cobd1 as a recovery disk.

What the script does, so the twenty minutes are not mysterious: creates the
filesystem, lays down the directory skeleton alpm needs before it will
initialise, installs about 790 packages, populates the pacman keyring (without
which every package fails verification as untrusted while its signature is
perfectly good), writes a real mirrorlist, runs ldconfig, and configures fstab,
autologin, networking, DISPLAY, the user and sudo.

The bootstrap problem, stated honestly: step 2 needs a Linux guest already
running, so this reproduces an image but does not create the first one.
`tools/mkmanjarorootfs.sh` is written to run on any Linux with pacman and
e2fsprogs, including a development machine, which is the path that removes the
circularity -- but it has only ever been run inside a guest, so that is a design
intent rather than a tested claim. `tools/mkrootfs.sh` builds the minimal
BusyBox image and is the other way in.
